  "live_records": {
    "q-49037": {
      "dom_id": "step-q49037",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "If ${f}^{′}(x)=|x-1|$ and $f(2)=3,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function  $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/270130d95065a37b075cb070da220bb774dc56bf096937cdba3755b3858615a1.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=1$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line $x=1.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(2)=3,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(1,2),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=2.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/995f590bbe6017ed49a4f62dad869c868343c1aa3f4aae026b636abb70e7246e.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and is increasing $↗$ on $(1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left of $x=1$ and goes up to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x=1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x=1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, $x=1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/f46b93b49d78fe2d7f5ee2dee238abab1cb3fbd1d505bca3bb2e903b3e770d92.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/0bc495bddd2a2082f17cd414f3e75c3c2a76b1fb934f7adecdc78f515b64efe2.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
