    "q-116633": {
      "dom_id": "step-q116633",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "If ${f}^{′}(x)=|2x+3|$  and $f(0)=3,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/0cbf67d9caa6e85f36b7ad49f187e3821212e118b4a95b953cf014165cfea09c.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{2})$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=3,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\frac{3}{2},0),$ the point at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ must be lower than the point at $x=0.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/5e6c757db49a8711f53d0df4782bbff8723fbb2efcc80f117695d990f69c2e91.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{2})$ and on $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left of $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and goes up to the right of $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/303d2b2398031ab460c607f28ee18a19df461b66b12aba26bd4faf0ed71d9177.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/b37706adef23aa3f0000e9e1bcad796d0e4911c70b25f459bad5cae6375982c5.png)",
