    "q-116888": {
      "dom_id": "step-q116888",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If ${f}^{′}(x)=|x+2|$ and $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function  $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/ca3cd9e4d10292e51f04d4bb1ea4837bed66b4264f108aaef1c26065079b36f9.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-2$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line $x=-2.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-2,0),$ the point at $x=-2$ must be lower than the point at $x=0.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/9a992dfb283f4ebd91eeaf79208348b68046b47c9b609379008df5bc6b0acae3.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and is increasing $↗$ on $(-2,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left of $x=-2$ and goes up to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x=-2$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x=-2$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, $x=-2$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/a6a14bbe875dd1af5fe52170894929ffe194a27da6e801847acf47f4f842a6e2.png)\n\n \n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14273358/assets/b5a189e6682782353e77fbf9b4db9bbf88392300097398323c52651359bdf3b9.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-116888-a",
